"""Plain-English notes for the most common weakness types (CWE), used by the weakness/<cwe-n> pages.

Each entry: CWE id -> (short name, what it is, how to prevent it). Kept short and factual; the pages add the live
CVE data. Only CWEs listed here get a page, so every page has hand-written context, not just a list.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

CWE = {
    "CWE-79": ("Cross-site scripting (XSS)",
               "The application puts attacker-controlled input into a web page without encoding it, so the attacker's script runs in other users' browsers. That script can steal sessions, act as the victim or rewrite the page.",
               "Encode output for its context (HTML, attribute, JavaScript, URL), use a templating engine that escapes by default, sanitize any HTML you must accept, and add a Content Security Policy."),
    "CWE-862": ("Missing authorization",
                "An action or resource has no check that the caller is allowed to use it, so any user (often any visitor) can call it directly. Common in plugin AJAX/REST endpoints.",
                "Check permissions on every request on the server side, deny by default, and test endpoints as a low-privileged and an anonymous user."),
    "CWE-416": ("Use after free",
                "Memory is used after it has been freed. An attacker who can shape what lands in that memory can often crash the program or run code. A classic browser and kernel bug.",
                "Use memory-safe languages or smart pointers, null out pointers after free, and run fuzzing with AddressSanitizer. For users: install vendor updates quickly."),
    "CWE-89": ("SQL injection",
               "User input is concatenated into a SQL query, so the attacker can change the query: read or modify data, bypass login, and sometimes run commands on the database server.",
               "Use parameterized queries or prepared statements everywhere, never build SQL from strings, use least-privileged database accounts, and validate input types."),
    "CWE-125": ("Out-of-bounds read",
                "The program reads past the end (or before the start) of a buffer. This leaks memory contents, such as keys or pointers, or crashes the process.",
                "Check lengths and indexes before reading, prefer bounds-checked APIs and memory-safe languages, and fuzz parsers."),
    "CWE-284": ("Improper access control",
                "The product does not correctly restrict who can access a resource or function. A broad category that covers missing, weak or bypassable access checks.",
                "Centralize access control, deny by default, enforce it on the server, and review every role and endpoint."),
    "CWE-863": ("Incorrect authorization",
                "There is an authorization check, but it is wrong: it checks the wrong thing, can be bypassed, or lets a user act on objects that are not theirs.",
                "Base decisions on the server-side identity and the specific object, test with multiple roles, and avoid trusting client-supplied roles or IDs."),
    "CWE-22": ("Path traversal",
               "A file path built from user input is not restricted, so sequences like ../ let the attacker read or write files outside the intended folder, such as config files, keys or source code.",
               "Resolve the canonical path and check it stays inside an allowed base directory, use allow-lists of file names or IDs, and run with minimal file permissions."),
    "CWE-639": ("Insecure direct object reference (IDOR)",
                "The app uses an ID from the request (like ?invoice=1043) to fetch an object without checking that it belongs to the user, so changing the ID exposes other users' data.",
                "Check ownership or permission for every object lookup, scope queries to the current user, and do not rely on hard-to-guess IDs alone."),
    "CWE-122": ("Heap-based buffer overflow",
                "Data is written past the end of a buffer on the heap. Attackers use it to corrupt memory and often to run code.",
                "Validate sizes before copying, use safe APIs and memory-safe languages, enable hardened allocators, and fuzz input handling."),
    "CWE-200": ("Information exposure",
                "The product reveals sensitive information, such as credentials, tokens, personal data or internal paths, to someone who should not see it.",
                "Classify sensitive data, return only what is needed, strip debug output and stack traces in production, and review API responses."),
    "CWE-918": ("Server-side request forgery (SSRF)",
                "The server fetches a URL supplied by the user, so the attacker can make it call internal services, cloud metadata endpoints (169.254.169.254) or other hosts behind the firewall.",
                "Allow-list destinations, block private and link-local ranges after DNS resolution, disable redirects, and require IMDSv2 on cloud instances."),
    "CWE-787": ("Out-of-bounds write",
                "The program writes past the bounds of a buffer. This is one of the most dangerous memory bugs, often leading to code execution.",
                "Bounds-check every write, use memory-safe languages or safe libraries, enable compiler hardening, and fuzz."),
    "CWE-119": ("Memory buffer errors",
                "Reads or writes go outside the bounds of a memory buffer. This is the parent class of buffer overflows and out-of-bounds access.",
                "Use bounds-checked APIs and memory-safe languages, enable stack protectors and ASLR, and patch promptly."),
    "CWE-306": ("Missing authentication",
                "A critical function can be used without logging in at all, such as an admin API, a management port or a debug endpoint.",
                "Require authentication on every sensitive function, keep management interfaces off the internet, and inventory exposed endpoints."),
    "CWE-94": ("Code injection",
               "User input ends up being evaluated as code (eval, template engines, expression languages), giving the attacker code execution inside the application.",
               "Never evaluate user input, use sandboxed template engines with auto-escaping, and allow-list any dynamic behavior."),
    "CWE-74": ("Injection",
               "Input is passed to another component (shell, query, template, interpreter) without neutralizing special characters, changing what that component does.",
               "Use APIs that separate data from commands (parameters, argument arrays), encode for the target, and validate input."),
    "CWE-78": ("OS command injection",
               "User input is placed into a shell command, so the attacker can append their own commands and run them on the server. Very common in routers, firewalls and other appliances.",
               "Avoid the shell: call programs with argument arrays, allow-list values, and never pass raw input to system(), exec() or backticks."),
    "CWE-20": ("Improper input validation",
               "The product does not properly check that input has the expected type, size, format or range, which opens the door to many other bugs.",
               "Validate on the server with allow-lists (type, length, range, format) and reject anything unexpected."),
    "CWE-400": ("Uncontrolled resource consumption (DoS)",
                "An attacker can make the product use up CPU, memory, disk or connections, slowing it down or taking it offline.",
                "Set limits and timeouts (request size, rate, recursion depth, connections) and test with hostile inputs."),
    "CWE-287": ("Improper authentication",
                "The product does not correctly verify who a user is, so the attacker can log in as someone else or skip login.",
                "Use proven authentication frameworks, enforce MFA, verify every step server-side, and test bypass paths."),
    "CWE-502": ("Deserialization of untrusted data",
                "The app deserializes attacker-supplied data (Java, .NET, PHP, Python pickle, YAML). Crafted objects can trigger code execution while being rebuilt.",
                "Do not deserialize untrusted input with native serializers, prefer plain JSON with schemas, and use type allow-lists if you must."),
    "CWE-770": ("Allocation without limits",
                "Resources are allocated without a cap, such as huge uploads, unbounded lists or decompression bombs, letting an attacker exhaust memory.",
                "Cap sizes and counts, stream large inputs, and limit decompression ratios."),
    "CWE-121": ("Stack-based buffer overflow",
                "Data overflows a buffer on the stack, which can overwrite the return address and hand control to the attacker. Frequent in embedded and IoT firmware.",
                "Use length-checked functions instead of strcpy or sprintf, enable stack canaries, NX and ASLR, and fuzz."),
    "CWE-269": ("Improper privilege management",
                "The product grants, keeps or fails to drop privileges correctly, letting a user or process gain more rights than intended.",
                "Apply least privilege, drop privileges early, separate roles, and audit privilege changes."),
    "CWE-476": ("NULL pointer dereference",
                "The program uses a pointer that is NULL, usually causing a crash (denial of service), and in some kernels more.",
                "Check return values and pointers before use, and fuzz error paths."),
    "CWE-190": ("Integer overflow",
                "An arithmetic result wraps around. Wrong sizes then lead to small allocations and buffer overflows.",
                "Use checked arithmetic, validate sizes before allocation, and prefer wider or unsigned types where appropriate."),
    "CWE-362": ("Race condition",
                "Two operations run at the same time on shared state without proper locking, so an attacker can win the race and break security assumptions.",
                "Use proper locking or atomic operations, avoid check-then-use patterns, and test under concurrency."),
    "CWE-352": ("Cross-site request forgery (CSRF)",
                "A logged-in user's browser can be tricked by another site into sending a state-changing request, such as changing settings or creating an admin.",
                "Require anti-CSRF tokens (nonces), use SameSite cookies, and check Origin on state-changing requests."),
    "CWE-434": ("Unrestricted file upload",
                "The app accepts uploads without checking the type, so the attacker uploads a script (like a .php web shell) and runs it on the server.",
                "Allow-list extensions and content types, store uploads outside the web root with random names, and never execute uploaded files."),
    "CWE-59": ("Link following",
               "The program follows a symlink or hard link planted by the attacker, so it reads or writes a file it should not, often as root.",
               "Open files with O_NOFOLLOW or equivalent, use safe temp-file APIs, and avoid privileged operations in user-writable folders."),
    "CWE-295": ("Improper certificate validation",
                "TLS certificates are not checked properly, so a man-in-the-middle can impersonate the server and read or alter traffic.",
                "Keep full certificate and hostname verification on, pin where appropriate, and never ship verify=False."),
    "CWE-77": ("Command injection",
               "Input changes a command that the application builds and runs. This is the general form of OS command injection.",
               "Separate commands from data (argument arrays), allow-list inputs, and avoid shells."),
    "CWE-285": ("Improper authorization",
                "The product does not check, or wrongly checks, whether the user may perform an action.",
                "Enforce authorization server-side for every action and object, and deny by default."),
    "CWE-345": ("Insufficient verification of data authenticity",
                "The product accepts data without checking that it really comes from a trusted source, such as unsigned updates, spoofable headers or unverified messages.",
                "Verify signatures or MACs on data that matters, and do not trust client-controlled headers for security decisions."),
    "CWE-347": ("Improper signature verification",
                "A cryptographic signature is not verified, or is verified incorrectly. JWT 'alg: none' and unchecked update signatures are typical examples.",
                "Use well-reviewed libraries, pin the expected algorithm and key, and reject anything unsigned."),
    "CWE-843": ("Type confusion",
                "Memory is treated as the wrong type of object. JavaScript engines (V8 and others) are a frequent source, and it is often exploited for code execution in browsers.",
                "Developers: use strict type checks and fuzzing. Users: update browsers quickly, because these bugs are often exploited as zero-days."),
    "CWE-601": ("Open redirect",
                "The app redirects to a URL taken from the request, which attackers use for convincing phishing links or to steal OAuth tokens.",
                "Redirect only to relative paths or an allow-list of hosts, and validate OAuth redirect URIs exactly."),
    "CWE-798": ("Hard-coded credentials",
                "A password or key is built into the product. Anyone who extracts it can log in to every installation.",
                "Never ship default or embedded secrets, generate unique credentials per device, and store secrets in a vault."),
    "CWE-532": ("Sensitive data in log files",
                "Passwords, tokens or personal data are written to logs that other people or systems can read.",
                "Redact secrets before logging, restrict log access, and review log statements in code review."),
    "CWE-732": ("Incorrect permission assignment",
                "Files, folders or resources get permissions that are too open, such as world-writable config or readable keys.",
                "Set least-privilege permissions explicitly at install and runtime, and audit them."),
    "CWE-522": ("Insufficiently protected credentials",
                "Credentials are stored or sent in a way that lets an attacker recover them, for example in plain text, with weak hashing or over HTTP.",
                "Hash passwords with a slow algorithm (Argon2, bcrypt), encrypt secrets at rest, and only send them over TLS."),
}
